---
title: Candied Jalapeños
description: Sweet pickled jalapeño rounds in a garlicky cider vinegar syrup, canned and left to mellow for two weeks.
tags: [pickles, canning]
course: condiment
icons: [pepper-hot, jar, candy]
author: Rebecca Lindamood
source:
  name: Foodie with Family
  url: https://www.foodiewithfamily.com/candied-jalapenos/
yield: 4-6%half-pints
prep time: 20m
cook time: 20m
---

> Wear gloves when handling the jalapeños.

Remove the stems from the @jalapeño peppers{3%lb}(fresh and firm, washed). The easiest way is to slice a small disc off the stem end along with the stem. Discard the stems.

Slice the peppers into uniform 1/8- to 1/4-inch rounds and set aside.

In a #large pot{}, bring the @cider vinegar{2%cup}, @white granulated sugar{6%cup}, @turmeric{1/2%tsp}, @celery seed{1/2%tsp}, @granulated garlic{3%tsp}, and @ground cayenne pepper{1%tsp} to a boil. Reduce the heat and simmer for ~simmer{5%minutes}.

Raise the heat to boiling again, add the pepper slices, and return to a hard boil. Reduce the heat again and simmer for exactly ~simmer{4%minutes}.

Use a #slotted spoon{} to transfer the peppers into clean, sterile #canning jars{}(half-pint or pint, with new two-piece lids), loading each to within 1/4 inch of the rim.

Turn the heat up under the pot of syrup and bring it to a full rolling boil. Boil hard for ~boil{6%minutes}.

Use a #ladle{} to pour the boiling syrup into the jars over the jalapeño slices. Push a #chopstick{} to the bottom of each jar two or three times to release any trapped pockets of air, and adjust the level of the syrup if necessary. Wipe the rims with a clean, damp paper towel and fix on the lids to fingertip tightness.

> **Extras** — There will likely be leftover syrup. Can it in half-pint or pint jars too, then brush it on meat on the grill or add it to potato salad.

Place the jars in a #canner{} and cover with water by 2 inches. Bring the water to a full rolling boil, then process for ~process{10%minutes} for half-pints or ~process{15%minutes} for pints.

Use #canning tongs{} to transfer the jars to a #cooling rack{}. Leave them to cool, undisturbed, for ~cool{24%hours}. When fully cooled, wipe the jars with a clean, damp washcloth and label them.

Let mellow for at least two weeks, but preferably a month, before eating.
